<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Pequot Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1638 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading power of 17th-century eastern Connecticut, whose defeat in the Pequot War of 1636 to 1638 opened southern New England to English expansion.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Pequot lived along the Thames and Mystic rivers in what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. By the early 17th century they had become the dominant people of the region, collecting tribute from neighbours and controlling the production and trade of wampum shell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-netherland">New Netherland</a> bought in exchange for cloth and metal goods.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Grand sachems such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatobem">Tatobem</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sassacus">Sassacus</a> ruled through councils of lesser sachems and fortified villages. Their wealth from the wampum trade, and their alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, made them targets: the Dutch killed Tatobem in 1633 or 1634, and rival sachem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uncas">Uncas</a> broke away to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohegan">Mohegan</a>. English settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> valley from 1633 brought rising tension.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1637 English forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-mason-connecticut">John Mason</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohegan">Mohegan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a> allies, burned the fortified village at Mystic, killing around 400 to 700 people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hartford-1638">Treaty of Hartford</a> in 1638 dissolved the nation. Survivors later regained reservations at Mashantucket and Lantern Hill.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1600 CE – 1638 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1638 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tatobem, Sassacus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Algonquian traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mohegan-Pequot</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pequot</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1619&amp;focus=pequot-nation" aria-label="Pequot Nation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2257 752 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pequot Nation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3231 946h0M3283 922h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2577 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2577 1008h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United States. <a href="/atlas?year=1619&amp;focus=pequot-nation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

